r i mean if you  can  import snow or rely on artificial snow  is that the end of the world  well i think you know this research is  just another way of illustrating this  problem which you know as i said is that  there is no aspect of our lives that  won't be touched by climate change if we  don't get it under control and i think  that research demonstrates that on our  current trajectory you know there won't  be anywhere in the world that can  actually host the winter olympics and  provide safe sustainable conditions for  athletes but you know that's as i said  one example we also in the uk already  know that communities here are  experiencing  more you know increased intense flooding  for example also a result of climate  change and we can't adapt our way out of  these impacts if we are  reaching you know levels of climate  change that are dangerous which is the  trajectory that we're on now we've got  to just address the root cause of the  problem which is greenhouse gas  emissions which are overwhelmingly being  driven by fossil fuels and you know the  uk is the second largest oil and gas  producer in europe we've got to move  away from those fossil fuels if we're  going to ensure that we not only have a  winter olympics but that we have a  livable climate more broadly  bob do you think that future sporting  events like this are under threat do you  think that skiing is under threat given  global warming and the beijing relying  almost entirely on artificial snow it's  going to use an estimated 49 million  gallons of water i mean what are the  implications in the future  well the difficult thing is that most of  the resorts and places where people  currently go for uh winter sports they  are relatively low levels and they're  they're there because they want to be  connected to make it easy for people to  arrive so the only way you're really  going to be able to find um  ways of doing winter sports with natural  snow is to move into places that are  less accessible higher up mountain  ranges further into the mountain ranges  but that would be a huge undertaking you  can't build a you know a ski resort and  uh winter sports venues so easily but  that's where we might go we have to go  move up in the mountain ranges and of  course that then increases the risk of  things like  altitudes dealing with the effects of  altitude so there's no quick fix this is  just another example as tesla's saying  of the long-term consequences of the  fact that the earth is warming and uh we  have to deal with the consequences yeah  and tessa does news like this when  you're talking about the winter games  does it actually get the message across  to people in a way that perhaps some  other warnings on climate change don't  when they can see a warning about the  future of the olympics  yeah i think it does in so far as you  know the olympics of a kind of universal  cultural event that no matter where you  live  um you've heard about you probably tune  into and the thought that after so many  decades um it may simply cease to exist  because we haven't gotten the climate  crisis under control is you know i think  yeah a way of making that quite tangible  but it is again only the tip of the you  know proverbial iceberg in terms of the  huge impact that the climate crisis is  going to cause if we don't get serious  about addressing its root causes okay  well we must leave it there but tessa  khan bob ward always interesting to get  your take on things thanks very much  indeed for tonight  and just to let you know that tomorrow  i'll be joined by climate researcher in  the application of artificial  intelligence to climate change and  founder of climate in color joycelyn  london and senior associate at the  energy and climate intelligence unit  richard black but for now that's it  thanks for watching i'll see you next  time  [Music]

