 lessons about business it covers so many  different areas doesn't it what's your  answer  yeah that's right anna and i think you  know as we've heard from the stories on  your program tonight there is no aspect  of our life that you know will not be  touched by climate change so it makes  sense to integrate it across a range of  subjects and i you know i have to say i  really admire the  future campaign and the leadership that  you know once again young people in the  uk are showing on the issue of how to  address the climate crisis how to  prepare for it um but i think you know  ultimately  the hard truth of the matter is that by  the time those young people are in  positions of real power a real political  power where they can make the decisions  that need to be made um to keep  you know climate change to a safe level  you know  that that moment will have passed and  what we need are the adults in the room  today the people who are in power our  governments the political decision  makers to actually do everything that  they can to keep warming to below that  critical threshold of 1.5 degrees and  not leave it to young people to continue  to shoulder the responsibility for this  crisis and we just haven't seen that you  know from this government which is  distracted by crises of its own making  when  when we are dealing with a climate  emergency and when the solutions to that  namely reducing greenhouse gas emissions  getting off fossil fuels are so clear  yeah and and we could go into a whole  different conversation couldn't we about  that and the government's saying we've  got some of the best targets in the  world as far as emissions are concerned  but let's stick to education if we can  and bob i mean how do you teach climate  change without scaring children  well that's an important question you  want uh people to be empowered by the  knowledge and  they play a very important role at the  moment i know that in many families it  is the children and and what they're  learning not just in school lessons but  also learning themselves  they are the drivers of understanding in  in families at the moment and getting  changes in their parents but one of the  most powerful lobbies i know in the  world are young um female students who  are in particular targeting their  fathers or mothers who are in senior  positions in business and telling them  you have to take this seriously so i  think they're very it's very very  important but people need to understand  that this is this is a living subject  and people need to feel empowered to do  they want to know what to do  and very quick thought from you tessa on  this this issue of you know some of the  pictures we see on our screen some of  the projections and the warnings are  quite scary  we have to handle the subject very  delicately don't we with younger people  well i mean anna you know again the fact  is that as was demonstrated by a study  that was published in the lancet late  last year what is really driving anxiety  among young people is not the facts of  climate change per se it's the sense of  betrayal that they feel that governments  aren't taking the action that's needed  even though governments have  acknowledged that we are in a climate  emergency so if we really care about the  mental health of young people we need to  focus on acting and you know acting at  the scale and pace that's required not  you know not running away from the  problem okay let's move on to our next  subject shall we and uh the winter games  in china will rely entirely on  artificial snow and a study this week  showed that only one of the last 21  winter olympics venues that sapporo in  japan will be suitable to host the event  in future if global warming continues on  its current trends  so how can winter games adapt to a  changing climate  tessa rising temperatures then are going  to have a real impact on host cities of  the  olympic games the winter games in future  but  how much does it matter i mean if you  can  import snow or rely on artificial snow  is that the end of the world  well i think you know this research is  just another way of illustrating this  problem which you know as i said is that  there is no aspect of our lives that  won't be touched by climate change if we  don't get it under control and i think  that research demonstrates that on our  current trajectory you know there won't  be anywhere in the world that can  actually host the winter olympics and  provide safe sustainable conditions for  athletes but you know that's as i said  one example we also in the uk already  know that communities here are  experiencing  more you know increased intense flooding  for example also a result of climate  change and we can't adapt our way out of  these impacts if we are  reaching you know levels of climate  change that are dangerous which is the  trajectory that we're on now we've got  to just address the root cause of the  problem which is greenhouse gas  emissions which are overwhelmingly being  driven by fossil fuels and you know the  uk is the second largest oil and gas  producer in europe we've got to move  away from those fossil fuels if we're  going to ensure that we not only have a  winter olympics but that we have a  livable climate more broadly  bob do you think that future sporting  events like this are under threat do you  think that skiing is under threat given  global warming and the beijing relying  almost entirely on artificial snow it's  going to use an estimated 49 million  gallons of water i mean what are the  implications in the future  well the difficult thing is that most of  the resorts and places where people  currently go for uh winter sports they  are relatively low levels and they're  they're there because they want to be  connected to make it easy for people to  arrive so the only way you're really  going to be able to find um  ways of doing winter sports with natural  snow is to move into places that are  less accessible higher up mountain  ranges further into the mountain ranges  but that would be a huge undertaking you  can't build a you know a ski resort and  uh winter sports venues so easily but  that's where we might go we have to go  move up in the mountain ranges and of  course that then increases the risk of  things like  altitudes dealing with the effects of  altitude so there's no quick fix this is  just another example as tesla's saying  of the long-term consequences of the  fact that the earth is warming and uh we  have to deal with the consequences yeah  and tessa does news like this when  you're talking about the winter games  does it actually get the message across  to people in a way that perhaps some  other warnings on climate change don't  when they can see a warning about the  future of the olympics  yeah i think it does in so far as you  know the olympics of a kind of universal  cultural event that no matter where you  live  um you've heard about you probably tune  into and the thought that after so many  decades um it may simply cease to exist  because we haven't gotten the climate  crisis under control is you know i think  yeah a way of making that quite tangible  but it is again only the tip of the you  know proverbial iceberg in terms of the  huge impact that the climate crisis is  going to cause if we don't get serious  about addressing its root causes okay  well we must leave it there but tessa  khan bob ward always interesting to get  your take on things thanks very much  indeed for tonight  and just to let you know that tomorrow  i'll be joined by climate researcher in  the application of artificial  intelligence to climate change and  founder of climate in color joycelyn  london and senior associate at the  energy and climate intelligence unit  richard black but for now that's it  thanks for watching i'll see you next  time  [Music]
 

